        explanation: "- Backstage is an open-source framework for building developer portals, built around a software catalog, software templates, TechDocs and a plugin ecosystem; it is a CNCF incubating project\n- The catalog holds entities — Components, APIs, Systems, Domains, Resources, Groups and Users — each described by a `catalog-info.yaml` in its repository, so ownership lives next to the code\n- Entity descriptors are discovered rather than entered: the portal ingests them from repositories, so the catalog updates through pull requests like anything else\n- The catalog's first job is answering ownership. 'Who owns this service?' is the question every incident, upgrade and security advisory begins with\n- Its second job is relationships: which component depends on which API, which resources a system consumes, what breaks when a database is retired\n- TechDocs renders documentation from Markdown in the repository, so docs are reviewed with the code and cannot silently rot in a wiki\n- Scorecards turn platform standards into visible, per-service checks: has an owner, has a dashboard, has an SLO, on a supported runtime version, no critical vulnerabilities\n- A portal is only as good as its data. A catalog that is 60% accurate is not consulted, and a catalog nobody consults stops being maintained — so automate ingestion and fail CI when a descriptor is missing or invalid\n- Keep the portal a read-and-act surface: see the service, its docs, its health, and take the two or three actions that matter (scaffold, deploy, request access)",
