        explanation: "- A platform API is the interface teams program against: a small set of high-level resources — a service, a database, a queue, an environment — with defaults, policy and ownership built in\n- Kubernetes-style control planes suit this because the reconciliation loop is continuous: the declared state is compared with reality forever, so drift is corrected rather than accumulating\n- The Operator pattern extends the API with custom resources and a controller that knows how to manage a specific workload — a database, a cache, a certificate\n- Crossplane turns the cluster into a control plane for external infrastructure: providers expose cloud services as managed resources, and compositions bundle them into one resource a team can request\n- In Crossplane v2 composite resources (XRs) are namespaced by default and claims are gone — teams interact with XRs directly, and platform machinery moved under `spec.crossplane`\n- Also in v2: composition functions are the only composition mechanism (native patch-and-transform is removed), pipelines run like a Job, and managed resource definitions let operators activate only the resources they need\n- Design the abstraction around intent, not around the cloud's parameters: `size: small | medium` and `retention: 7d` rather than forty passthrough fields\n- Always expose an escape hatch — a documented way to set an unusual parameter, or to drop to raw resources — because a platform API that cannot express a real requirement gets bypassed entirely\n- Return status honestly: ready, provisioning, failed with a reason, plus the connection details. A resource that says nothing forces developers back into the cloud console",
